Generic type aliases — same-file
================================

A generic type alias `type Pair[A, B] = ...` is a pure
type-level abbreviation: it has no codegen of its own; each
use site substitutes the arguments and the body is treated
as if written inline.

This file pins the same-file form. Cross-file generic
aliases live in `generic_alias_cross_file.lsysl`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

Alias to a function type — fully instantiated at use site
---------------------------------------------------------

A `(T) -> T` alias names a closure shape. Functions taking
this alias at a concrete instantiation accept any closure of
the right shape — there is no nominal newtype, so no cast is
required.

    type GabUnary[T] = (T) -> T

    apply_unary_int(f: GabUnary[int], x: int) -> int = f(x)

    #test
    test_alias_function_type() -> unit
        val r = apply_unary_int(x -> x + 1, 41)
        assert(r == 42, s"42 should be 42, got $r")

Two-parameter alias
-------------------

    type GabBin[A, B] = (A, A) -> B

    use_bin(f: GabBin[int, int], a: int, b: int) -> int = f(a, b)

    #test
    test_alias_two_params() -> unit
        val r = use_bin(_ + _, 10, 20)
        assert(r == 30, s"10+20 should be 30, got $r")

    // TODO: re-enable once the analyzer accepts a generic-alias instantiation
    // in a struct field-type position. Currently:
    //
    //     struct GabPipeline
    //         f: GabUnary[int]
    //
    // fails with "'GabUnary' is not a generic type" while the *exact same*
    // instantiation as a function parameter type (above) succeeds. The bug is
    // therefore a field-type resolution gap, not an alias gap. Filed in the
    // ROADMAP under the "known gaps" section.
